<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kirkuk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kirkuk is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, on the site of ancient Arrapha, capital of a Hurrian kingdom in the 15th and 14th centuries BCE. Its citadel mound has been occupied for millennia. The discovery of oil at nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baba-gurgur">Baba Gurgur</a> in 1927 made it the centre of Iraq's northern oil industry, and its mixed Kurdish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> and Arab population has made its status disputed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kirkuk.jpg" alt="Kirkuk" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chad.r.hill at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kirkuk_citadel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kirkuk lies 238 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, on the ruins of the original Kirkuk Citadel near the Khasa River. Findings at the Shanidar Cave settlement suggest that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthals</a> occupied the area, and a large quantity of pottery of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubaid-period">Ubaid period</a> has been excavated from several tells in the city. Its ancient name was the Hurrian Arrapha. Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> it was known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> as the Citadel of the House of Seleucid, and the surrounding region appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> sources as Beth Garmai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Arrapkha belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> and was exposed to raids by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lullubi">Lullubi</a> in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a>. Around 2150 BCE it was occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutians">Gutians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a> dwellers speaking a language isolate, and it was the capital of the short-lived kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutium">Gutium</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a> drove the Gutians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> around 2115 BCE. In the 1450s BCE the Mittani attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and brought Arrapkha under their control, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-assyrian-empire">Middle Assyrian Empire</a> overthrew them in the mid-14th century BCE.</p><p>Arrapha then became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> proper, the Hurrian population was driven away, and the city rose to prominence in the 11th and 10th centuries BCE and remained important under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a>. In the Parthian and Sassanid periods it was the capital of the small Assyrian state of Beth Garmai.</p><p>Arab Muslims conquered the region in the 7th century CE. Hasanwayhid and Annazid Kurds ruled Kirkuk from 1014 to 1120, then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took it in 1258, after which it belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>. In 1851 it came under direct Ottoman rule. The British occupied Kirkuk on 7 May 1918. Because both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> wanted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilayet">Vilayet</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, to which Kirkuk belonged, the question went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a>, and under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Angora</a> of 1926 Kirkuk became part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><p>The discovery of vast quantities of oil in the region after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> was the impetus for annexing the vilayet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Pipelines from Kirkuk run through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> to Ceyhan on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, with two parallel lines built in 1977 and 1987.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kirkuk is the capital of the Kirkuk Governorate in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and has a diverse population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a>, Iraqi Turkmens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, with smaller numbers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>. A late 19th-century source gave it about 30,000 inhabitants, and in the early 20th century Turkish was the most common language spoken at home. Its monuments include the Kirkuk Citadel, the Tomb of the Prophet Daniel and the Qaysareyah of Kirkuk. The archaeological sites of Qal'at Jarmo and Yorgan Tepe lie on the outskirts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kirkuk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Arrapha, the ancient name of <b>Kirkuk</b>, became the capital of a Hurrian kingdom east of the Tigris in the 15th century BCE, when the region fell within the sphere of the Mitanni Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-arrapha">Kingdom of Arrapha</a></li><li><blockquote>The League awarded the Mosul region to Iraq in 1925, confirmed by treaty with Turkey in 1926, and oil was struck at Baba Gurgur near <b>Kirkuk</b> in 1927.</blockquote><a href="/state/mandatory-iraq">Mandatory Iraq</a></li></ul></section>
